==============================================================================
THE DECAY INTERMEDIATE -- the central finding
==============================================================================
  at B4 T4096 HV32 K128 C64 (fla's BT=64, BC=16, NC=4), bf16:
    tinygrad  [B,NT,HV,C,C,K]      = 4,294,967,296 elements = 8.00 GiB, whole batch
    triton    [BC,K] per sub-chunk = 2,048 elements, registers only
    triton    whole-grid set       = 67,108,864 elements = 0.125 GiB, all of it L1/registers

    >>> RATIO  256x  <<<

    derivation, and it is a property of the two programs, not the GPU:
      tinygrad, per (b, chunk, head) : C  x C  x K = 64x64x128 = 524,288
      triton,  per sub-chunk         : BC x K       = 16x128 = 2,048
      C^2 / BC = 4096/16 = 256
      Whole grid, the triton side never exceeds B*NT*HV*NC*BC*K = B*NT*HV*C*K
      because exp2 of the shifted difference is applied to the operand as it
      is loaded, so the ratio is at least 64x even there.
      256x is the tight per-program figure: the one to quote.

    the same ratio at every shape in this run (it does not move):
      B4 T4096 HV32 K128 C64  ->  256x   (8.00 GiB materialised)
      B4 T1024 HV8 K64 C64  ->  256x   (0.25 GiB materialised)
      B2 T512 HV4 K64 C64  ->  256x   (0.03 GiB materialised)

==============================================================================
throughput -- same GPU, same shapes, both implementations
==============================================================================
  device Tesla T4   backend tinygrad:CUDA
  every ms is  total = build + issue + kernel + copy
    build  = python graph construction, no device work
    issue  = realize(): the scheduler linearises the unrolled graph, then
             launches. On CUDA the launches are async, so issue is CPU time
             and is usually dominated by tinygrad's python scheduler.
    kernel = time spent waiting for the device after that sync point
    copy   = .numpy(), the device-to-host readback
  the graph is rebuilt inside the timed region; realize() on an
  already-realized graph is a silent no-op -- see the probe above

  shape                       1 interm     peak   fla ms  tiny ms   ratio
